  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive breakdown of the word **INDOLENT**:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd6 Definition\n\n**Indolent** means *averse to activity, effort, or movement; habitually lazy.*\n\nIt is an **adjective** meaning slow or lazy. More specifically, it describes a tendency to avoid work, exertion, or activity, often due to a preference for ease and comfort \u2014 suggesting a lack of motivation or a reluctance to put in effort, resulting in a passive or lazy attitude towards tasks and responsibilities.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 Medical Usage\n\nThe word also has a specialized meaning in medicine. Doctors use the word *indolent* to describe medical conditions that are **slow to progress**. If you're diagnosed with an illness, you'd prefer an indolent one over one that spreads quickly.\n\nIn pathology, it can also mean *causing little pain*, or (especially of a painless ulcer) *slow to heal*.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd24 Part of Speech\n- **Adjective** (e.g., *an indolent person*, *an indolent tumor*)\n- The noun form is **indolence** (e.g., *\"Some argue that television encourages indolence.\"*)\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf31 Etymology (Word Origin)\n\nThe word comes from **Late Latin** *indolent-, indolens*, meaning \"insensitive to pain,\" from Latin *in-* + *dolent-, dolens* (present participle of *dol\u0113re*, meaning \"to feel pain\"). The main modern sense of \"laziness, love of ease\" dates to around 1710, perhaps reflecting the notion of avoiding trouble.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcac Example Sentences\n- *\"The indolent employee spent more time chatting than working.\"*\n- *\"Despite the pressing deadline, he maintained an indolent pace, unconcerned about the consequences.\"*\n- *\"Her indolent lifestyle was a stark contrast to her energetic childhood.\"*\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd01 Synonyms\nSynonyms include: **lazy, slack, idle, slow, sluggish, inactive, inert, languid, lethargic, listless, lackadaisical, torpid, slothful.**",
